index e8afe5a..2c35005 100644
--- a/docs/configuration.md
+++ b/docs/configuration.md
@@ -836,7 +836,9 @@ when it expires. Reusing a key supersedes its previous value atomically.
 Manage records without starting a model, from the project directory:
 
 ```sh
-kiln memory export                 # JSON, including correction history
+kiln memory export > memory.json   # JSON, including correction history
+kiln memory import memory.json --dry-run
+kiln memory import memory.json --on-conflict skip
 kiln memory forget test-runner     # deletes every version of this key
 kiln memory restore 12             # restores this project's version as a new record
 ```
@@ -845,11 +847,31 @@ Explicit user preferences can be shared across projects:
 
 ```sh
 kiln memory global remember response-style "Prefer concise answers"
-kiln memory global export
+kiln memory global export > preferences.json
+kiln memory global import preferences.json --on-conflict replace
 kiln memory global forget response-style
 kiln memory global restore 12
 ```
 
+Import reads exactly one export-format JSON array from a file (up to 4 MiB and
+1000 rows). It validates the entire history but imports only the highest-ID
+version of each key, assigning new IDs and creation times; it does not replay
+exported correction history. Import always targets the current project, or the
+explicit `global` preferences scope, regardless of scopes in the file. By
+default any existing active key (including expired or stale ones) rejects the
+whole batch. `--on-conflict skip` leaves existing keys untouched; `replace`
+creates a new correction of each existing key. `--dry-run` checks the same
+input, sources, and conflicts and reports what would happen without changing
+memory. Both flags can follow the filename. Success prints JSON counts for
+`inserted`, `replaced`, `skipped`, and `dry_run`.
+
+Project file sources are rebound by their original root-relative paths into
+the current worktree and must match the destination files' SHA-256 hashes.
+Missing, changed, escaping, nonregular, or oversized files reject the entire
+import, even when attached only to historical rows. Global imports accept
+preferences without sources only. Imports never read paths on the exporting
+machine or contact a model.
+
 The global scope accepts preferences only, without project file dependencies.
 Model memory tools cannot write to it. Automatic retrieval searches project
 records first, then global preferences within the same record and token limits.
